        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Fortunately, this problem can be resolved by the following observation: The final rounding decisions for column i are only affected by updates performed on this very column, and so updates to later columns are irrelevant at this point in the process. This makes it possible to “lazily batch” updates together, thus achieving much better GPU utilization. Concretely, we apply the algorithm to  $ B = 128 $ columns at a time, keeping updates contained to those columns and the corresponding  $ B \\times B $ block of  $ H^{-1} $ (see also Figure 2). Only once a block has been fully processed, we perform global updates of the entire  $ H^{-1} $ and W matrices using the multi-weight versions of Equations (2) and (3) given below, with Q denoting a set of indices, and  $ H_{-Q}^{-1} $ denoting the inverse matrix with the corresponding rows and columns removed:",
            "block_bbox": [
                208,
                223,
                1013,
                424
            ],
            "block_id": 2,
            "block_order": 2,
            "group_id": 2,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    208.0,
                    223.0
                ],
                [
                    1013.0,
                    223.0
                ],
                [
                    1013.0,
                    424.0
                ],
